ESP32-based LED mapping

Overview:
Enable mapping of LEDs attached to an ESP32 board.

1. Port the pattern generator to embedded.
2. Enable a webserver on the ESP32 serving the client-side code (probably just pointing the phone at a resource hosted elsewhere, since the client-side code is large).
3. Webserver on the ESP32 implements the API required to sync pattern clock for mapping.
4. Add support for counting LEDs as well (user can solder whatever length to the output channels on the ESP32--there should be an algorithm that as a first stage uses the camera to detect how many LEDs there are on each strip; could be done by binary-searching with color blocks)
5. Once LEDs are counted/mapped, user can optionally run skeletonization (see https://github.com/fughilli/graph-extractor)
6. LED mapping + skeletonization data are sent to embedded system over webserver
7. ESP32 then runs patterns on array (configurable via webserver as well, similar to WLED). If skeletonized, the "pulse" pattern should be used (see graph-extractor for the reference implementation; this will need to be ported to an efficient real-time embedded library in Rust)

For embedded support, use https://github.com/fughilli/embedded

All heavy-lifting computationally should be performed on the phone. The embedded system should only have to do the timing-critical control of the LEDs and the pattern generation (none of the solver arithmetic).
